===============================================================================
LINUX /etc/shadow FILE
===============================================================================
1. OVERVIEW & PURPOSE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
In early Unix/Linux implementations, user account attributes and encrypted 
password hashes were stored together in /etc/passwd. Because /etc/passwd 
requires world-read permissions (0644) so processes can map User IDs (UIDs) 
to usernames, any user could extract password hashes and execute offline 
dictionary or brute-force attacks (e.g., using John the Ripper or Hashcat).

To eliminate this vulnerability, modern Linux systems isolate authentication 
hashes and password aging policies into the /etc/shadow file.

* File Ownership:   root:shadow (or root:root depending on distribution)
* Permissions:      0600 (-rw-------) or 0640 (-rw-r-----)
* Access Scope:     Strictly restricted to root or processes operating with 
                    the CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE capability (e.g., PAM modules).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. FILE SYNTAX & FIELD BREAKDOWN
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Each line in /etc/shadow represents a single user account and contains 
9 colon-separated (:) fields:

username:encrypted_password:last_change:min_age:max_age:warning:inactivity:expiration:reserved

Sample Record:
sysadmin:$6$qZ7yX2aP$k9/3.9wXb...:19800:0:90:7:14:19999:

Field Descriptions:
1. Username:
   Matching account login name in /etc/passwd.
   Example: sysadmin

2. Encrypted Password:
   Hashed password string (Prefix + Salt + Hash) or account status flag.
   Example: $6$qZ7yX2aP$k9/3.9wXb...

3. Last Password Change:
   Days elapsed between Jan 1, 1970 (Unix Epoch) and last modification.
   Example: 19800

4. Minimum Password Age:
   Minimum days required before user is allowed to change their password.
   Example: 0 (Can change immediately)

5. Maximum Password Age:
   Maximum days password remains valid before mandatory password reset.
   Example: 90 (Expires in 90 days)

6. Warning Period:
   Number of days warning issued to the user prior to password expiration.
   Example: 7 (Warn 7 days before)

7. Inactivity Period:
   Days after expiration before account is permanently locked/disabled.
   Example: 14 (Disable 14 days post-expiration)

8. Expiration Date:
   Absolute date (in epoch days) when the entire account expires.
   Example: 19999

9. Reserved:
   Reserved field for future features (currently unused/blank).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. CRYPTOGRAPHIC HASH FORMATS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Field 2 uses Pluggable Authentication Modules (PAM) crypt-compatible 
hashing algorithms identified by a $id$ prefix:

Identifier  | Algorithm        | Status / Recommendation
------------+------------------+-----------------------------------------------
$1$         | MD5              | Obsolete / Insecure
$2a$ / $2y$ | Blowfish (bcrypt)| Secure (BSD/Debian variant default)
$5$         | SHA-256          | Secure
$6$         | SHA-512          | Current Standard
$y$         | yescrypt         | Modern Enterprise Standard (Fedora, RHEL, etc)

Special Characters in Field 2:
* "!" or "*": Account locked/disabled for password login (e.g., system daemons).
* "!" prefix: Account locked via `usermod -L` or `passwd -l`.
* Empty (""): No password set (High security risk).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. ADMINISTRATIVE COMMANDS & MANAGEMENT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
To edit the file safely with write locks:
  $ sudo vipw -s

Password Aging Management (chage):
  # View account aging details
  $ sudo chage -l sysadmin

  # Set max age 90 days, warn 14 days prior, inactivity 7 days
  $ sudo chage -M 90 -W 14 -I 7 sysadmin

  # Set absolute account expiration date (YYYY-MM-DD)
  $ sudo chage -E 2026-12-31 sysadmin

Locking and Unlocking Accounts:
  # Lock account
  $ sudo usermod -L sysadmin

  # Unlock account
  $ sudo usermod -U sysadmin

Integrity Audit:
  # Verify formatting and cross-reference with /etc/passwd
  $ sudo pwck -r

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. SECURITY & HARDENING RECOMMENDATIONS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Enforce strict permissions:
   $ sudo chmod 600 /etc/shadow
   $ sudo chown root:root /etc/shadow

2. Audit for accounts without passwords:
   $ sudo awk -F: '($2 == "") { print $1 }' /etc/shadow

3. Set system default aging rules in /etc/login.defs:
   PASS_MAX_DAYS   90
   PASS_MIN_DAYS   10
   PASS_WARN_AGE   7
